From 55c27d6ae46dbc0bc32997da296cb7d206145784 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Hargett <plaztiksyke@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 07:23:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Colour a shadow after its blur, and run a lone colour-space
 conversion in the layer composite

The shadow pass coloured the source's coverage with the shadow colour
before the blur, in 8 bits premultiplied. A channel that is a fraction of
a step there - a dark colour in linearRGB - rounded to a whole step or to
none before the blur spread it. Canvas 2D sets the colour on the blurred
alpha, and so do Firefox and Chromium's written-out SVG shadow chains. The
coverage is now filled opaque white under SourceIn, so the image holds the
source's alpha, and the shadow colour tints the blurred image as the
composite lands, rounded once.

A layer whose filter chain is a single LinearRgbToSrgb or SrgbToLinearRgb,
without a blend mode, now converts each texel as its composite samples it,
rounded to 8 bits as the conversion's pass stores its result. The pixels
are the pass's; the layer reserves no result image and no filter work. A
group shadow merged in linearRGB is two such layers, so it saves two
full-layer images and two passes. The image paint carries the conversion
in a uniform slot that was free (frag[13].y). The sRGB transfer curve is
one function per shader language, shared by the pass and the paint.

Tests: a shadow coloured after its blur (fails on the previous pass); a
lone conversion's composite against the pass arithmetic, both directions;
the layer's reservation and the composite's paint.
